= Verification =

== Overview ==
'''Verification''' is the process of checking or confirming, using appropriate evidence or formal procedure, that specified requirements, properties, calculations, implementations, records, or claims are satisfied.

The exact meaning of verification depends on the domain. In systems engineering it usually concerns conformity with specified requirements. In formal methods it can mean mathematically proving that a system satisfies a formal specification. In source checking it can mean confirming that a source exists and actually supports an attributed claim.

Verification is therefore not a universal synonym for "true."

== Engineering verification ==
In systems and software engineering, verification asks whether a product, component, or work product satisfies specified requirements or design characteristics.

The common shorthand is:

<pre>
verification → "Was it built right?"
validation   → "Was the right thing built?"
</pre>

This shorthand is useful but simplified. Real engineering verification can involve:
* inspection;
* analysis;
* demonstration;
* test;
* formal proof;
* review.

The applicable method depends on what requirement is being verified.

== Verification and validation ==
[[Validation]] is closely related but distinct.

Verification typically compares something against an explicit specification.

Validation evaluates whether it is suitable or effective for an intended use, target, or application.

A system can be verified against its written requirements and still fail validation if the requirements did not capture the real need.

Conversely, a prototype may appear useful in practice while still failing formal verification against a complete specification.

== Software verification ==
Software verification asks whether software satisfies relevant specifications or properties.

Methods include:
* code review;
* unit and integration testing;
* static analysis;
* model checking;
* theorem proving;
* type checking;
* symbolic execution;
* runtime assertions.

Testing can reveal defects but normally cannot prove the absence of all possible defects.

Formal methods can prove particular properties relative to a formal model and assumptions.

== Formal verification ==
'''Formal verification''' uses mathematical methods to establish that a formal model or implementation satisfies a formal specification.

Examples include:
* model checking finite-state systems;
* theorem proving;
* proof assistants;
* verified compilation;
* refinement proofs.

A formal proof can still fail to guarantee real-world correctness if:
* the specification is wrong;
* the model omits important behavior;
* assumptions about hardware or environment are false;
* the implementation differs from the verified artifact.

Formal verification therefore shifts, rather than eliminates, questions about [[Validation|validation]] and [[System boundary|system boundary]].

== Scientific-model verification ==
In modeling and simulation, verification commonly asks whether the computational model correctly solves or implements the intended mathematical model.

For example, a fire simulation can be verified for:
* numerical implementation;
* conservation equations;
* convergence;
* code correctness.

That does not establish that the mathematical model accurately represents real fires. That question belongs to model validation.

== Source verification ==
Verification can also apply to sources and records.

A source-verification check may ask:
* Does the cited source exist?
* Is it authentic?
* Does it contain the attributed claim?
* Is the quoted passage accurate?
* Is the cited version the relevant one?

This verifies the citation/source relation, not the truth of every claim inside the source.

== Identity and integrity verification ==
Digital systems may verify:
* hashes;
* signatures;
* certificates;
* credentials;
* transaction inclusion;
* file integrity.

Cryptographic verification can establish mathematical relations such as possession of a key or integrity of signed data.

It cannot automatically establish that a human statement or external-world event encoded in the data is true.

== Verification and reproducibility ==
[[Reproducibility]] can act as a form of verification for computational work.

Re-running the same code and data can verify that the reported output is reproducible under the stated environment.

It does not by itself validate the scientific interpretation or prove the original inputs were correct.

== Verification evidence ==
A strong verification record states:
* what was verified;
* against which requirement/specification;
* by which method;
* on which artifact/version;
* under which assumptions;
* with what result.

The label "verified" without this context is weak information.

== BITwiki application ==
BITwiki uses verification within [[BITwiki:Claim validation]] and [[BITwiki:Provenance]] to check source existence, attribution, transformations, and review history.

That application is one use of the broader concept.

